  "worked_solution": "Let $N$ be the event that a randomly selected person doesn't smoke, and let $F$ be the event that they are female. Then, we require $P(F|N),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(F|N)=\\frac{P(N|F)P(F)}{P(N)}=\\frac{P(N|F)P(F)}{P(N|F)P(F)+P(N|{F}^{′})P({F}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since $17%$ of all females and  $38%$ of all males smoke, we have\n\n$$\nP(N|F)=1-0.17=0.83,\\,P(N|{F}^{′})=1-0.38=0.62.\n$$\n\n- Since $51%$ of the population are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.51,\\,P({F}^{′})=1-0.51=0.49.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|N) & =\\frac{P(N|F)P(F)}{P(N|F)P(F)+P(N|{F}^{′})P({F}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.83)(0.51)}{(0.83)(0.51)+(0.62)(0.49)} \\\\  & ≈58%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest percent.",
